<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
      The application's first and only NETWORK permission.

      feature-pairing/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml says where this belongs and why it was not added
      earlier: "INTERNET belongs to Phase 3 (the sync engine), and adding it early would retire the
      'this app talks to nobody' property months before anything actually needs it." This is Phase 3,
      and this is that module. The app's other permissions (CAMERA for QR pairing, and the biometric
      pair) open no socket; until this line nothing in the app could reach the network at all.

      Declared HERE, in the one module that contains network code, on the same principle CAMERA
      follows: the permission exists because of this module and disappears with it. A build that
      drops :core-sync-net drops the permission with it and needs no manifest edit. The manifest
      merger lifts it into the merged application manifest, so there is no second declaration.

      This file is in src/androidMain/ rather than src/main/ because the module is now Kotlin
      Multiplatform (Android + JVM, so the desktop app can use the same transport). That is the
      whole of the move: androidMain is the Android target's source set, the manifest is still this
      module's, and the merged application manifest is unchanged.

      What it actually permits, and when:

      1. Nothing happens until the user has both paired a device and configured a server URL. No
         code in this module runs on its own: there is no scheduler here, no WorkManager, no boot
         receiver and no Service. Every request is made by a caller that already holds a
         ServerEndpoint the user supplied, and this build has no such caller yet.

      2. The only host contacted is that URL. There is no fallback endpoint, no discovery, no update
         check and no bundled default address, and redirects are refused so a response cannot move
         the client to a different host.

      3. There is deliberately NO analytics, crash reporting, telemetry or advertising dependency in
         this module, and adding one here would change what this permission means. Every
         third-party dependency in core-sync-net/build.gradle.kts is a transport: Ktor's client and,
         underneath it on Android, the OkHttp engine. Neither reports anything anywhere, and neither
         contacts a host this module did not ask it to.

      4. Nothing readable leaves the device. Record bodies are sealed by core-crypto before they
         reach this module, which treats them as opaque bytes and has no code that could open one.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

</manifest>
